en.description:
**Goal**: the inverting summing amplifier gives Uo = −(RF/R1 × U1 + RF/R2 × U2). A switch provides a DC input and the function generator an AC input; the output is their sum (inverted).

**Circuit** (±9 V dual supply, wired as in Experiment 2-7):
- IN+ to GND, RF (10kΩ) from IN− (the summing node SUM) to OUT;
- input 1: slide switch S1, side a to +9V, side b to GND, com → R1 (47kΩ) → SUM;
- input 2: function generator FG1 (black clip to GND) → R2 (10kΩ) → SUM.

**Predict first**: SUM is a virtual ground (0 V), so the two input currents I1 = U1 / R1 and I2 = U2 / R2 don't disturb each other; they meet at SUM and all of it flows through RF (KCL): Uo = −RF (I1 + I2). Work out the DC part with S1 on a and the gain for the signal, then predict the output in the three cases below.

**Measure** (generator at 1 kHz sine, scope on the output, multimeter DC range for currents):
1. S1→b, amplitude 1 V;
2. S1→a, amplitude 1 V;
3. S1→a, generator set to amplitude 0.5 V with a 1 V offset; also measure the DC currents in R1 and R2. Once the circuit check passes, type your readings into the Measurements below (whatever your own board shows); the worked comparison appears after you answer.

**Think about it**: this is the basic idea behind audio mixers, level shifters and digital-to-analog converters (DACs). Because the summing node is a virtual ground, adding another input doesn't affect the others; that is what makes an op-amp summer better than just tying signals together with resistors.

**In a real circuit**: the output range is again V− to V+ − 1.5 V (the simulator has this too): in case 3 the output only goes down to about −2.91 − 0.5 ≈ −3.4 V, far from −9 V, so nothing clips. A real LM358 slews at about 0.3–0.5 V/µs, which the simulator does not model; a few volts at 1 kHz are not affected.

en.compare:
**Compare**: DC part = −10k × 9 / 47k ≈ **−1.91 V**; signal part = −10k / 10k × u_fg = **−u_fg** (gain −1).
1. AC only, the output is ≈ **1.99 Vpp** with a 0 V average (not 2.00 Vpp because of the generator's 50Ω output resistance, see Experiment 2-7);
2. still 1.99 Vpp, but the whole waveform moves down to between **−0.92 V and −2.91 V**, average −1.91 V;
3. output average ≈ **−2.91 V** = −(1.91 + 1.00). R1 ≈ 0.19 mA, R2 ≈ 0.10 mA on average, together 0.29 mA, all of which flows through RF (from SUM toward OUT).
